#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171834 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/id #uuid "1ecd84f8-5157-595a-93b6-a865acaa53d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8378" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n![](images/3b/3bf74b3f4c11508b9daa66464cc451933f5637c22ffeaaae303378fca2571893.png)\nand the curve $\\mathcal C,$ defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal C:\\: 2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+ 2x_2^2 -2x_1+2x_2 =7 .\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(\\mathbf{x})=2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+2x_2^2$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $5y_1^2-y_2^2,$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $\\mathcal C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\: \\underbrace{ 2x_1^2+6x_1x_2+2x_2^2}_{Q(\\mathbf{x})} -2x_1+2x_2 =7\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n![](images/78/783b32a7595534982e5fb057c049081564807b9d47988670c7d30058fb3900a0.png)\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(\\mathbf x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $\\mathcal C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2})+2(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2}) & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{2}}{y}_{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable $y_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}) & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2})+2 & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-({y}_{2}^{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{y}_{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2})+(\\sqrt{2})^{2} & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+2 & =7 \\\\ 5{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}_{1}^{2}-{({y}_{2}-\\sqrt{2})}^{2} & =5 \\\\ 5{z}_{1}^{2}-{z}_{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171835 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/id #uuid "c03c6fbb-176a-5f30-b928-73908cb93277" :question/math-academy-id "e-8380" :question/problem "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ![](images/a4/a49d63b985288c12f1f2e6494534c6d69a52b52cb19b15ff716c5893aaaadbf8.png)-plane?\n\n- ![](images/1f/1f9c6caabac759264990b2d2d23f2dd2a0960a721cbf57e0cc2a405ff5d800c6.png)\n- ![](images/c7/c77f3bd2dde7986a670e81dae93439c2a2be22fc736738a90130b3a82d8027e5.png)\n- ![](images/4e/4e6827d9bc7fcfbcc59953380c1ddbefbf1537adaf4b539c2286338cd001fc06.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.  \n\n- The equation ![](images/61/61e7a29736499942ecd74ac3f1cde7f58b5c1c88624cccf698f8d5dff0151b0e.png) defines a non-empty set. It represents the point   ![](images/63/63658ff56cb42b62a8aff0dd6883e53dc63e0c7d115acf571e96ed9f63421029.png) in the ![](images/38/38801c8aee855aeef6e7490e7ce6ba196b64443ab6d7032313628f818afb4a79.png)-plane.\n\n- The equation ![](images/95/951eb118d8f44d67994f51091397fc57d8e330053ef011d0cc23bcac40f5cea2.png) defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ![](images/61/612b633b99184f47ee064ada5f6d901b484b0fe6853cb3a8cb4d90417b5ae99d.png) and ![](images/1e/1e5a2da0565685a7cc228dfa89095c1ef454bbb8b62e9806759223af8b85deec.png) while the right-hand side is negative.\n\n- The equation ![](images/54/546424b4f70a30ccbef3b6e8add2050617bd83978407c00cf88afa63e8f22b5f.png) defines a non-empty set. It represents a  pair of intersecting lines, ![](images/a2/a25e48e9d48f10c78491a71883fbc73e8637ccecafa9f194d0943c77fac46da0.png) and ![](images/df/df4ee978ef8c5458e021927c04566092af64c7f60f3aa1f4c2534e19c2f173be.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171836 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172030 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/id #uuid "58f6a3e7-e867-576a-ad84-0debbbc38d90" :question/math-academy-id "e-8381" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n3x_1^2 + 2x_1 - x_2 =\\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the variable $x_1\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1})-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2})-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & ={x}_{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &={x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2} & ={y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive $y_2$-axis."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86538b9a-7568-5933-8854-c5b4658f48fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468942} {:answer/id #uuid "7015220a-f9e7-5df7-8058-44ffcd7f8d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7015220a-f9e7-5df7-8058-44ffcd7f8d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468943} :answer-field/id #uuid "1042346b-f6d0-5bb9-9f5f-bce0a807b4a3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470083} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec93fa16-4cde-5228-9add-72224548a4b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468944} {:answer/id #uuid "f37fedd9-c61b-5bc4-9e9b-a9572e1053ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f37fedd9-c61b-5bc4-9e9b-a9572e1053ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468945} :answer-field/id #uuid "862e7db4-e4cf-5fa7-beaa-ce2dadf572fd" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470084} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8f6328b-6e1c-5742-b270-297de5ba0087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468946} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2783d8-30e3-52a0-8485-391ca3a4046f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f2783d8-30e3-52a0-8485-391ca3a4046f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468947} :answer-field/id #uuid "b91acfa1-f88a-5755-910e-8b8e92796d63" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7ec5e81-6115-5881-b6f7-16bb9226da5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140554" :question/problem "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?\n- **I.** ${x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}=-1$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}=0$\n- **III.** ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}=-1$ defines a non-empty set. It represents the hyperbola ${x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2}=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set. It represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the negative ${x}_{2}$-axis in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set. It represents a single point $(0,0)$ in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172030 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34b2f2de-15d8-5ab1-b00e-502c92c80ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365019} {:answer/id #uuid "4a07a491-9472-50b7-8caa-2551ff3dffe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365020} {:answer/id #uuid "d3feab1f-710d-5f7d-916d-6dccaf02ac63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365021} {:answer/id #uuid "626e74b3-c07c-5db1-8452-88dfd54f5e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365022} {:answer/id #uuid "83cebc46-9365-5662-a211-861041f69667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "626e74b3-c07c-5db1-8452-88dfd54f5e59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365022} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa02b4c6-e130-5f57-94ca-7875e4b08f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59ff20d8-dfd7-5f57-8dd7-676de1c62793" :question/math-academy-id "q-140557" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1}=-7\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variable ${x}_{1}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}^{2}+16{x}_{1} & =-7 \\\\ 4({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+4-4) & =-7 \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+2)^{2}-16 & =-7 \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+2)^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variable\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+2,\n$$\n\nwe get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}_{1}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the pair of parallel lines ${y}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${y}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff7e060b-1316-5b07-b470-df5ef86724ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{10}$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366617} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c4007f-a76f-5450-9d48-38785eca112a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366618} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb12856-ee48-5173-af4f-f74503391dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366619} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d1a16b-ebea-596a-b098-e79157afe072" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366620} {:answer/id #uuid "43f64814-08c5-5d87-94c2-ca5d06ca5917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $\\sqrt{5}$ and $2\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3c4007f-a76f-5450-9d48-38785eca112a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366618} :answer-field/id #uuid "204f44fd-2c19-5e76-8874-9a7fd60fd729" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c86266f7-94c1-52a6-b432-a64ea0eb634a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95391" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=40.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=40\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2} & =40 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2}+\\sqrt{10}(-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2})+3\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2}) & =40 \\\\ 15{y}_{1}^{2}+5{y}_{2}^{2}+10{y}_{2} & =40 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2} & =8 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+({y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{2}+1) & =8+1 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}+1)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}+1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}_{1}^{2}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{{z}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{3}$ and $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c75de21-c317-5237-86ad-e88e440edb77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404810} {:answer/id #uuid "84aa3f18-3554-54fd-ae6f-b39ef7e2bffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404811} {:answer/id #uuid "6840aa9b-2e07-5c84-8108-3e6b1673c189" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404812} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa9bf77-da54-502a-8bbc-e943b623178f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404813} {:answer/id #uuid "41613630-2c51-5ec0-9667-5e99088f87b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41613630-2c51-5ec0-9667-5e99088f87b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of parallel lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404814} :answer-field/id #uuid "394f4d35-8eb9-5319-8076-2545b5e06b52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c9fcd9c7-a9e3-5124-83fe-aab4333a0614" :question/math-academy-id "q-95399" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}-6\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $10{y}_{1}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+2\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}-6\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly: \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+9{x}_{2}^{2}+2\\sqrt{10}{x}_{1}-6\\sqrt{10}{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 10{y}_{1}^{2}+2\\sqrt{10}(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2})-6\\sqrt{10}(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{y}_{2}) & =0 \\\\ 10{y}_{1}^{2}-20{y}_{1} & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{1}+1)-1 & =0 \\\\ {({y}_{1}-1)}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({y}_{1}-1)}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n{z}_{1}={y}_{1}-1,\n$$\n\nwe get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the pair of parallel lines  ${z}_{1}=1$ and ${z}_{1}=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172028 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26871e46-51ca-5680-a064-bcee4ac984fd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a26ebc2d-f69b-5be5-a62d-fe9979a136f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171352} {:answer/id #uuid "a7226782-b0b8-5d75-83ac-7f6e8629c23f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle of radius $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171353} {:answer/id #uuid "c22bc215-d13d-59ed-b376-a61232d50fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171354} {:answer/id #uuid "3ab9b53c-e1b2-5002-844a-b3352768d4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171355} {:answer/id #uuid "575bdd47-ebf1-53ed-9f53-e33ef9a63b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171356}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d5f2036a-0fe0-50c3-b684-fe037208bf4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171351} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55e68231-d9b2-5aa6-aa84-112d0e369b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $1$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382609} {:answer/id #uuid "c5acccd0-f6c1-52dc-be77-7297d98e1951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A circle of radius $\\sqrt{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382610} {:answer/id #uuid "584525fe-a6ca-5466-a0ac-0215dce4e93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382611} {:answer/id #uuid "a71f511b-3fb2-554e-b7b1-c0c673c47df4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382612} {:answer/id #uuid "b2730ecd-1e31-5200-83ad-515fcbe8bd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "584525fe-a6ca-5466-a0ac-0215dce4e93d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382611} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a166c0-af1b-5c41-95f1-54242ee0704a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ba048324-ec24-5cc4-9fc3-2455f8f53e00" :question/math-academy-id "q-95400" :question/problem "Consider the change of variables given by the matrix equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand the curve $C,$ defined as\n\n$$\nC:\\,2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}-2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-26.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven that the change of variables above reduces $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}$ to its canonical (diagonal) form $6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2},$ what type of a quadratic curve is defined by $C?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note the following:\n\n$$\nC:\\,{{2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}+4\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}-2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2}=-26\n$$\n\nLet's write the given change of variables explicitly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} &=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2},\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe know that the given change of variables reduces $Q(x)$ to its canonical form. Therefore, performing the change of variables on $C,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{5}{x}_{1}-2\\sqrt{5}{x}_{2} & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+4\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2})-2\\sqrt{5}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{y}_{2}) & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-10{y}_{2} & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we complete the square for the variable ${y}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-10{y}_{2} & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+({y}_{2}^{2}-10{y}_{2}+25)-25 & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}-5)}^{2}-25 & =-26 \\\\ 6{y}_{1}^{2}+{({y}_{2}-5)}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1} &={y}_{1}, \\\\ {z}_{2} &={y}_{2}-5,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{z}_{1}^{2}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}_{1}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}})^{2}}+{z}_{2}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis defines an empty set, also called an imaginary ellipse. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${z}_{1}$ and ${z}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172624 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9390f3e1-b0fa-5c8e-b3b2-8f128e01d352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C_{3}: x_{2}^{2} = 2x_{1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171370} {:answer/id #uuid "af9c7b40-1bd3-528e-99ef-9cb011ef3954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C_{1}: x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171371} {:answer/id #uuid "b23b10c3-2a35-5668-9c93-f32a05d68efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C_{5}: x_{1}^{2} + \\frac{x_{2}^{2}}{4} =-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171372} {:answer/id #uuid "ef8ab7d5-bef5-595e-b321-a52275ce5452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C_{4}: x_{2}^{2} = 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171373} {:answer/id #uuid "37530859-5aa8-5aaa-8aca-cd0f1a6ab337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C_{2}: \\frac{x_{1}^{2}}{4} - \\frac{x_{2}^{2}}{9} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171374}] :answer-field/id #uuid "39cdf548-2580-5d02-8060-91eecdc48cb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186171369}] :question/id #uuid "7fe38749-b027-5e25-83ce-123ba8294cb8" :question/math-academy-id "q-95701" :question/problem "Which of the following equations defines two parallel lines in the $x_{1}x_{2}$-plane?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f74e5cac-5fa1-5913-b097-bbfbb558a4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477491} {:answer/id #uuid "b04d30a9-862b-5922-aa61-4c21080e6261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b04d30a9-862b-5922-aa61-4c21080e6261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477492} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e9331d7-eb32-5848-a41a-7768e9e3c9dd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477715} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "739f9b2d-f525-52a6-9c4c-b5f7a895148d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477493} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb66722-f6bc-5c27-b93b-33f61576756f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "739f9b2d-f525-52a6-9c4c-b5f7a895148d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477493} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad460fe6-a6b8-5d19-8fcd-46fae2f7d401" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477716} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb480cd5-e9a4-517a-b0d5-4fcec4bff81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477495} {:answer/id #uuid "8a869b7d-6beb-5fe3-93e8-d4e81ef106e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb480cd5-e9a4-517a-b0d5-4fcec4bff81d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477495} :answer-field/id #uuid "854812e6-d405-5148-8484-67f195d21ee7" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "098d9a26-30b9-593f-93e1-d543e66fd2ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-95703" :question/problem "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane?\n- **I.** ${x}_{1}^{2}=6$\n- **II.** ${x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{5}=-1$\n- **III.** ${x}_{2}^{2}=-7$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}=6$ defines a non-empty set. It represents two parallel lines, ${x}_{1}=-\\sqrt{6}$ and ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{6}.$\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{5}=-1$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{2}^{2}=-7$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-negative for all ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172030 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9905cbf0-c114-5744-928d-549e3bb50896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366727} {:answer/id #uuid "9e16170d-702e-59ac-b59e-0c7868a93b97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366728} {:answer/id #uuid "80dbb7a6-0d32-5adc-8510-5b0aaf2c2838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366729} {:answer/id #uuid "8f72c2ee-efca-59b0-87ff-0f833655ad91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366730} {:answer/id #uuid "7a752195-1acf-532a-b58a-41a03894c235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f72c2ee-efca-59b0-87ff-0f833655ad91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366730} :answer-field/id #uuid "66665b04-8cc0-542f-ad9b-e8d5d6255ced" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d74d7da-0e93-513c-8a0b-cbe7ece164cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-96067" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=9\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variables ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} & =9 \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1})-({x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{2}) & =9 \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}+1-1)-({x}_{2}^{2}-6{x}_{2}+9-9) & =9 \\\\ 3({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-3-({x}_{2}-3)^{2}+9 & =9 \\\\ 3({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-({x}_{2}-3)^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &={x}_{1}-1, \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2}-3,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}_{1}-1)^{2}-({x}_{2}-3)^{2} & =3 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{{\\sqrt{3}}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $\\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172030 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49b74a69-2c2c-5286-a987-fee946fd48b6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09493249-579f-50d6-83f8-d9f4339e49f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A single point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404873} {:answer/id #uuid "d2aec024-bd0a-5a8b-adfb-0e939c2e2b2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola with semiaxes $1$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404874} {:answer/id #uuid "78bda88f-4c26-582a-bfaf-2eedaeccc00e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A pair of intersecting lines" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404875} {:answer/id #uuid "e6eb1689-82d7-59cd-87a7-f9f401986eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semiaxes $2$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404876} {:answer/id #uuid "d21ffd0e-5191-5fa0-b217-96304319a883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d21ffd0e-5191-5fa0-b217-96304319a883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404877} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd5aaa14-82c5-5b2c-8c17-18030cfdb9c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9d0bc05c-84b7-5858-84df-86a5d9c6d43e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96084" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{2}=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variable ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}^{2}-4{x}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}+1-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2({x}_{2}-1)^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ 2({x}_{2}-1)^{2} & =-({x}_{1}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &={x}_{1}-2, \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{2}^{2} & =-{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the negative ${y}_{1}$-axis."}]}
